INT. FUNHOUSE MIRROR MAZE - NIGHT

Cracked glass. Dead carnival. Flashlight beams ricochet everywhere.

BIRDIE OKAFOR, 30s, tool belt, glitter on one cheek, scrapes at a mirror frame. Up a ladder.

DEZ PRYCE, 20s, sweaty intern in a clown wig, "holds" the ladder.

BIRDIE
Dez. Ladder.

DEZ
Holding it, Birdie.

The ladder wobbles. Dez grips harder. Wobbles more.

BIRDIE
You hold it like a baby bird.

DEZ
You're named Birdie. That's on you.

BIRDIE
Squeegee. Now.

Dez hands up a rubber chicken.

BIRDIE (CONT'D)
Chicken. Not squeegee.

DEZ
They were next to each other!

Reflections. Six Dezes. Six Birdies. Total chaos.

BIRDIE
Which one's you?

DEZ
(to all six)
Hi.

Footsteps. Squeaky soles. A pie, held like a weapon.

FERENC VASS, 70s, tuxedo jacket over pajamas, owner of everything rotting here, shuffles in.

FERENC
Who is breaking my maze?

BIRDIE
Fixing, Mr. Vass. Fixing your maze.

FERENC
Dez, is that my wig?

DEZ
Borrowed, Mr. Vass.

FERENC
Birdie, I pay for repairs. Not for humming.

BIRDIE
I wasn't humming.

DEZ
She was. Loudly.

Ferenc offers the pie. Nobody takes it. Everybody looks at it.

A flashlight beam, bright and official, slices through the mirrors.

INSPECTOR TAMSIN WHITLOCK, 40s, clipboard, zero humor, steps in.

TAMSIN
Fire marshal. Who's in charge?

All three point at each other. Six Dezes point. Six Birdies point.

FERENC
(re: the pie)
Pie?

TAMSIN
Obviously.
